  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh amplitude longwave trough on pace to pivot across the region \nthis evening. Strong cold front attendant to the main height fall \ncenter noted accelerating southeast across the local area this \nafternoon. A few pockets of showers will continue to emerge over the \nnext several hours as the frontal slope and trailing cold air \nadvection engage sufficient resident moisture depth. Improvement in \nmixing depth within the immediate wake of this front will also \nprovide a greater window for gusts to exceed 30 mph during the \nevening hours, particularly across the northern thumb. A secondary \nbrief lake effect response likely off lake Huron, as a short window \nfor meaningful shower production occurs within a zone of convergence \nalong the eastern thumb through at least the first half of the night \nbefore low level flow veers sufficiently to direct activity \noffshore. Progressive clearing trend through the back half of the \nnight as the notably colder/drier post-frontal environment gains \ntraction with time. Latest NBM guidance offers a general spread in \nprojected low temperature Saturday morning ranging from sub-freezing \nin the Saginaw valley to mid 30s portions of metro Detroit and near \nthe immediate shoreline locations. While a persistent low level \ngradient may prove more detrimental at times for frost formation, in \ncollaboration with neighboring offices still operating within the \nend of the growing season, will elect to highlight appropriate \nfrost/freeze headlines based on projected low temperature. \n\nSeasonably cold airmass entrenched to start the weekend period, \nmarked but sub-freezing temperatures at 850 mb. Surface ridging in \ncontrol beneath prevailing mid level northwest flow affords deep \nlayer dry and stable conditions, translating into a high degree of \ninsolation potential. Despite this, existing thermal profile will \nsimply support highs of lower 50s - or a solid 10 degrees below \naverage. Light winds emerging from the southwest Saturday night as \nthe surface ridge slips east. This may offer some limitation to \nradiational cooling potential, but with minimum temps Sunday morning \nstill arriving in the mid 30s given the magnitude of the existing \ncolder profile. This will afford some areas of frost. Ensuing \nwarming trend starting Sunday as existing low level southwest flow \nestablish a pattern of warm air advection beneath a building upper \nheight field. The strength of the advective process will bring \ntemperatures back closer to average for mid October. \n\nA high magnitude deep layer dry and stable environment will exist \nthrough much of the upcoming work week, as conditions remain \ngoverned by low and mid level ridging. This ensures precipitation \nfree conditions during this time. Moderating thermal profile \nmagnified by existing upper ridging will lend to above average \ntemperatures throughout this period. Highs mid 60s to around 70 \ndegrees, with minimum temps in the 40s.",
